    "data_processing_and_feature_engineering": null,
    "model_design": "Change only WEIGHT_DECAY = 0.0 to WEIGHT_DECAY = 0.00001, letting the frozen host's existing Adam implementation apply its L2 regularization without modifying the optimizer or adding a loss term. Preserve the supplied current architecture exactly: position-free tied item embeddings, eight independent blocks with dilations [1,2,4,8,1,2,4,8], gated local/dilated branches, learned per-channel residual scales initialized to 0.1, causal padding, normalization, GELU, dropout, initialization, masking, and candidate-scoring logic; historical results motivate retaining these features but do not establish that weight decay will improve them. Keep MAXLEN=128, HIDDEN_UNITS=64, NUM_BLOCKS=8, KERNEL_SIZE=3, DROPOUT_RATE=0.2, LR=0.001, and BATCH_SIZE=128 unchanged, preserving every existing variable name and constructor, forward, encode_sequence, and score_candidates signature and behavior. Retain exactly the three permitted imports, eight numeric constants, and NextItNetBlock and NextItNetModel classes with methods only, adding no file/network access, printing, training, evaluation, or CSV generation. Allocate one candidate within the total 44-slot budget for the unchanged host protocol—training seed 2024, validation sampling seed 42, identical splits and 200 validation users with 99 negatives each, BPR loss, Adam, clipping, 30 epochs, checkpoint selection, and candidate scoring—and compare validation HR@10 with 0.645, treating 0.655 as an unmeasured target representing two additional hits and consulting no test metric.",
    "training_evaluation": null
  }
